<exercises xml:id="ex-6-1-First_look_polynomials" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title component="not-workbook">Exercises</title>

	<xi:include href="ex-intro.ptx" />

    <exercise xml:id="gravel-path-first-look-polynomials">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Urban community gardens are catching on. What was once an
                abandoned lot down the block is now a thriving 10' <m>\times</m>
                25' vegetable and berry garden for the neighborhood. (Remember '
                stands for <q>feet,</q> so the garden is 10 feet wide and 25
                feet long.) One neighbor volunteered to donate gravel to make a
                path around the garden. The path will be 3 inches deep and the
                same width all around.

                <image>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[line width=1.5pt, scale=.5]
                            \fill[gray!20] (0,0) rectangle (1,1);
                            \fill[gray!20] (9,0) rectangle (10,1);
                            \fill[gray!20] (0,4) rectangle (1,5);
                            \fill[gray!20] (9,4) rectangle (10,5);
                            \fill[gray!20] (1,0) rectangle (9,1);
                            \fill[gray!20] (1,4) rectangle (9,5);
                            \fill[gray!20] (0,1) rectangle (1,4);
                            \fill[gray!20] (9,1) rectangle (10,4);
                            \draw (0,0) rectangle (10,5);
                            \draw (1,1) rectangle (9,4);
                            \draw (1,0) -- (1,1);
                            \draw (9,0) -- (9,1);
                            \draw (1,4) -- (1,5);
                            \draw (9,4) -- (9,5);
                            \draw (0,1) -- (1,1);
                            \draw (0,4) -- (1,4);
                            \draw (9,1) -- (10,1);
                            \draw (9,4) -- (10,4);
                            \node at (-.75, 4.5) {$W$};
                            \node at (0.5, 5.75) {$W$};
                            \draw [\lt -, \gt =stealth, line width=0.8pt] (1, 3.25) -- (4, 3.25);
                            \node at (5, 3.25) {25'};
                            \draw [-\gt , \gt =stealth, line width=0.8pt] (6, 3.25) -- (9, 3.25);
                            \draw [\lt -, \gt =stealth, line width=0.8pt] (2.25, 1) -- (2.25, 2);
                            \node at (2.25, 2.5) {10'};
                            \draw [-\gt , \gt =stealth, line width=0.8pt] (2.25, 3) -- (2.25, 4);
                            \node at (6, 2) {GARDEN};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
                <em>Story also appears in 2.3 Exercises 2.4 Exercises and 3.5 #4</em>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The other measurements are in feet, so convert the depth of the path (3 inches) into feet also.  
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose for the moment that the path will be 4 feet wide. Calculate the area of the path.  Here's one way to do it:  first, find the area of the outer rectangle, then subtract the area of the garden itself.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Hint: the length of that outer rectangle includes the 25 feet of garden plus the width of the path on each side. Same for the width of that outer rectangle.</em>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Figure out how much gravel they would need (in cubic feet)
                    for a 4 foot wide path by multiplying your answers to (a)
                    and (b).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Actually, they aren't sure how wide the path should be and
                    how much gravel they can get. Let's write <m>W</m> = width
                    of path (feet). Explain why the width of the outer
                    rectangle is <m>10+2W</m> and the length of the outer
                    rectangle is <m>25+2W</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the area of the path is
                    <m>(10+2W)(25+2W)-250</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's write <m>G</m> = amount of gravel (cubic feet).
                    Write an equation for <m>G</m> as a function of <m>W</m>.
                    Hint: multiply your answers to (a) and (e).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use FOIL and collecting like terms to simplify your answer to (f).  It should fit the quadratic equation template.  
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Notice that if <m>W=0</m>, then <m>G=0</m>.  Explain what that means in terms of the story.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="sticker-packs-first-look-polynomials">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Emery is selling custom printed laptop and water bottle sticker
                packs. He is trying to understand how the number of sticker
                packs people will buy depends on the price. There's a fancy name
                for the number of things people will buy <ndash/> it's called
                the <alert>demand</alert>. Emery knows from experience that if
                he prices sticker packs at $10, then the demand is 200 sticker
                packs, but that the demand drops by 15 sticker packs per dollar
                increase in price. He identifies the variables
                <md>
                  P = \text{ price of sticker packs (\$) }\sim \text{ indep}
                </md>
                <md>
                  D = \text{ demand (sticker packs) }\sim \text{ dep}
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many sticker packs will Emery sell if he sets the
                      price at $11? $12? $13?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Make a table showing how demand depends on price,
                    including <m>P= 10, 11, 12, 13</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the increase in price is calculated as
                    <m>P-10</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write an equation for the demand in terms of price. Hint:
                    Your equation will involve <m>(P-10)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the distributive property and collect like terms to
                    simplify your equation. Hint: it is linear.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Check that your simplified equation gives <m>D = 200</m>
                    when <m>P = 10</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What does your equation say the demand will be when
                    <m>P=0</m>? This answer does not make sense in the story.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Draw a graph illustrating how the demand depends on the
                    price. Be sure to start at $0.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <xi:include href="../GraphPaper.ptx" />
        </task>

    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="sticker-pack-revenue-first-look-polynomial">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In the previous problem, Emery was selling sticker packs. Using
                the variables
                <md>
                  P = \text{ price of a sticker pack (\$) }\sim \text{ indep}
                </md>
                <md>
                  D = \text{ demand (sticker packs) }\sim \text{ dep}
                </md>
                Emery calculated that (spoiler alert)
                <md>
                  D = 350-15P
                </md>
                Notice that the intercept (price $0) of 350 things doesn't
                really make any sense in the problem. Emery wants to figure out
                a price for sticker packs so that he can take in as much money
                as possible. There's a fancy name for that too <ndash/> the
                total amount of money Emery takes in is called the
                <alert>total revenue</alert>.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the total revenue if Emery prices sticker packs at
                    $10 and sells 200 of them?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Make a table showing how revenue depends on price,
                    including <m>P= 10, 11, 12, 13</m>. Hint: use your answers
                    for demand from the previous problem.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write an equation for the revenue in terms of price, using
                    <md>
                    R = \text{ total revenue (\$) }\sim \text{ dep}
                    </md>
                    Hint: use your final equation from the previous problem.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the distributive property and collect like terms to
                    simplify your equation. Hint: it is quadratic.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Check that your simplified equation gives <m>R = 2000</m>
                    when <m>P = 10</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What does your equation say the revenue will be when
                    <m>P=0</m> and what does that mean in the story?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Draw a graph illustrating how the demand depends on the
                    price. Be sure to start at $0.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <xi:include href="../GraphPaper.ptx" />
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="energy-drink-first-look-polynomials">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Ximena is trying to get some homework done so she wants to
                create a custom 24 ounce energy drink that has some caffeine,
                but not too much. She has two available drinks that she can mix:

                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      Rehab Monster: Green Tea
                      <aside>Source: <url href="https://www.monsterenergy.com/en-us/energy-drinks/rehab-monster/green-tea/">https://www.monsterenergy.com/en-us/energy-drinks/rehab-monster/green-tea/</url></aside>
                      (10.3 mg caffeine/ounce)
                    </p>
                  </li>

                  <li>
                    <p>
                      Snapple Peach Tea
                      <aside>Source: <url href="https://snapple.com/products/snapple-peach-tea?filter=tea">https://snapple.com/products/snapple-peach-tea?filter=tea</url></aside>
                      (2.3 mg caffeine/ounce)
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
                She is interested in the variables
                <md>
                  M = \text{ amount of Rehab Monster (ounces) }\sim \text{indep}
                </md>
                <md>
                  C = \text{ amount of caffeine (mg)}\sim \text{ dep}
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If Ximena uses 10 ounces of Rehab Monster, how many ounces
                    of Snapple Peach Tea will she use (remember she wants 24
                    ounces total) and how much caffeine will that mixture have?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   If she uses 16 ounces of Rehab Monster, how many ounces of
                    Snapple Peach Tea will she use and how much caffeine with
                    that mixture have? 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write an equation describing how the total caffeine is a
                    function of the amount of Rehab Monster. Hint: How can we
                    write the amount of Snapple Peach Tea in terms of
                    <m>M</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the distributive property to simplify your equation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>

    <exercise component="not-workbook" xml:id="canvas-painting-first-look-polynomial">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Roy is mounting a <m>69'' \times 48''</m> rectangular canvas
                painting on an aluminum composite panel. The panel covers the
                entire back of the painting and sticks out width <m>W</m> inches
                around all four sides of the canvas.
            </p>
            <image>
                <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.15]
                    \draw[thick, fill=gray!20] (0,0) rectangle (69,48);
                    \draw[thick, fill=white] (4,4) rectangle (65,44);
                    \node at (23,30) {\textbf{PAINTING} };
                    \draw[\lt -\gt ] (0,16) -- (4,16) node[midway,above] { $W$};
                    \draw[\lt -\gt ] (46,0) -- (46,4) node[midway,right] { $W$};
                    \draw[\lt -\gt ] (4,16) -- (65,16) node[midway,above] { 69"};
                    \draw[\lt -\gt ] (46,4) -- (46,44) node[midway,right] { 48"};
                \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose for the moment that the frame creates a border 3
                    inches wide ( <m>W = 3</m> ). Calculate the area of the
                    aluminum panel. Note: the area is the area of the outside
                    square, not just the border.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Roy has not decided yet how wide the frame border should
                    be. Let's write
                    <m>W = \text{width of the frame border (inches)}</m>.
                    Explain why the width of the outer frame is <m>69 + 2W</m>
                    and the length of the outer frame is <m>48 + 2W</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the area of the aluminum panel is
                    <m>(69 + 2W)(48 + 2W)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use FOIL and collecting like terms to simplify your answer
                    to (c). It should fit the quadratic equation template.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the area if <m>W=0</m>? Explain what that means in
                    terms of the story.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise component="not-workbook" xml:id="cold-brew-first-look-polynomials">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Viviana is selling bottled cold brew coffee at a local weekend
                market. She is trying to understand how the number of bottles
                people will buy depends on the price per bottle. Viviana knows
                from experience that if she prices cold brew at $4.00 per
                bottle, the demand is 150 bottles, but for every $1 increase in
                price, the demand drops by 20 bottles. She writes the variables:
              <md>
                P = \text{price of a cold brew bottle (\$) }\sim \text{indep}
              </md>
              <md>
                D= \text{demand (bottles) }\sim \text{dep}
              </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many bottles will Viviana sell if she sets the price
                    at $4.50? $5.00? $5.50?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Make a table showing how demand depends on price,
                    including <m>P = 4.00, 4.50, 5.00, 5.50</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain what <m>P-4</m> represents in the story.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write an equation for demand in terms of price. Hint: Your
                    equation will involve subtracting the demand drop from
                    150.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the distributive property and collect like terms to
                    simplify your equation. Hint: it is linear.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Check that your simplified equation gives <m>D = 150</m>
                    when <m>P = 4</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise component="not-workbook" xml:id="cold-brew-2-first-look-polynomials">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In the previous problem, Viviana was selling bottled cold brew
                coffee. Using the variables:
                <md>
                P = \text{price of a cold brew bottle (\$) }\sim \text{indep}
              </md>
              <md>
                D= \text{demand (bottles) }\sim \text{dep}
              </md>
              she wrote an equation showing how the demand depends on the
                price.
              </p>

              <p>
                Viviana wants to figure out a price for cold brew so that she
                can take in as much total revenue as possible.
              </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the total revenue if Viviana prices cold brew at
                    $4.00 and sells 150 bottles?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Make a table showing how revenue depends on price,
                    including <m>P = 4.00, 4.50, 5.00, 5.50</m>. Hint: use
                    your answers for demand from the previous problem.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write an equation for the revenue in terms of price, using
                    <m>R = \text{total revenue (\$) }\sim \text{dep}</m>.
                    Hint: use your final equation for demand <m>D</m> from the
                    previous problem and the fact that
                    <m>\text{Revenue}= \text{Price}\times \text{Demand}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the distributive property to simplify your equation.
                    Hint: it is quadratic.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Check that your simplified equation gives <m>R = 600</m>
                    when <m>P = 4</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise component="not-workbook" xml:id="liquid-nutrient-first-look-polynomials">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Květa is working in a botany lab and needs to prepare a
                30-gallon batch of liquid nutrient solution for a greenhouse
                experiment. She has two brands of concentrates available to mix:
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      <alert>Hofner's:</alert> contains 12 grams of nitrogen per
                      gallon.
                    </p>
                  </li>

                  <li>
                    <p>
                      <alert>DeBoer's:</alert> contains 4 grams of nitrogen per
                      gallon.
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </p>

              <p>
                She is interested in the variables:
              <md>
                <mrow>H \amp= \text{amount of Hofner's concentrate used (gallons) }\sim \text{indep}</mrow>
                <mrow>N\amp= \text{total mass of nitrogen in the mixture (grams) }\sim \text{dep}</mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If Květa uses 10 gallons of Hofner's concentrate, how many
                    gallons of DeBoer's concentrate will she use (remember she
                    needs 30 gallons in total), and how many total grams of
                    nitrogen will that mixture contain?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If she uses 18 gallons of Hofner's concentrate, how many
                    gallons of DeBoer's concentrate will she use, and how many
                    grams of nitrogen will that mixture contain?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write an equation describing how the total grams of
                    nitrogen <m>N</m> is a function of the amount of Hofner's
                    concentrate <m>G</m>. Hint: How can we express the amount
                    of DeBoer's concentrate used in terms of <m>H</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the distributive property and collect like terms to
                    simplify your equation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

</exercises>